\lispPackageChapter{livermore/time}

The \texttt{livermore/time} package names the fields of a Common Lisp
decoded time, parses and formats dates, and computes Julian and ordinal
dates.  Most functions take an optional \lispVar{time}, which may be a
universal time or a decoded-time list.  The default is the current time.

\section{Decoded Time}

\lispGenericSubsection{decoded-time-list}

Returns \lispVar{time} as the nine-value list produced by
\texttt{decode-universal-time}.  An integer is decoded; a list is returned
unchanged.

\lispFunctionSubsection{get-decoded-time-list}

The current time as a decoded-time list.

The accessors \texttt{time-second}, \texttt{time-minute}, \texttt{time-hour},
\texttt{time-date}, \texttt{time-month}, \texttt{time-year},
\texttt{time-day}, \texttt{time-daylight-p}, and \texttt{time-zone} are the
nine fields in order.  \texttt{time-day} is the weekday, Monday~$=0$ through
Sunday~$=6$.  \texttt{time-month-name} and \texttt{time-month-short-name}
are English names.  \texttt{time-quarter} is $1$ through $4$.

\lispFunctionSubsection{month-to-number}

The month number for an English name, January~$=1$.  Short names such as
\texttt{"Jan"} are accepted.

\lispFunctionSubsection{month-name}

The full English name for a month number.

\lispFunctionSubsection{month-short-name}

The abbreviated English name.

\lispFunctionSubsection{encode-month-year}

The universal time of the first of \lispVar{month} in \lispVar{year}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{encode-year}

The universal time of 1~January of \lispVar{year}.

\section{Parsing}

\lispFunctionSubsection{yyyy-mm-dd-to-universal-time}

Parses a string of the form \texttt{YYYY-MM-DD}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{dd-month-yyyy-to-universal-time}

Parses a string of the form \texttt{DD-Mon-YYYY} or \texttt{DD-Monthname-YY}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{date-string-to-universal-time}

Chooses between those two by the length of the middle field.

\texttt{dd-month-yyyy-to-decoded-time} and
\texttt{dd-month-yyyy-to-decoded-time-list} decode the same strings.

\section{Julian and Ordinal Dates}

\lispFunctionSubsection{time-julian-day-number}

The Julian Day Number at noon for a Gregorian date.  Years work as
1~\textsc{Bc}~$=0$, 2~\textsc{Bc}~$=-1$, and so on.  Also called
\texttt{time-jdn}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{time-julian-date}

The Julian Date, including the fraction of the day past noon.  Also called
\texttt{time-jd}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{time-ordinal-date}

The day of the year, $1$ through $366$.  Also called
\texttt{time-day-of-year}.

\section{Formatting}

\begin{description}
\item[\texttt{yyyy-string}] \texttt{"2020"}
\item[\texttt{qqyyyy-string}] \texttt{"1Q2020"}
\item[\texttt{qq-yyyy-string}] \texttt{"Q1-2020"}
\item[\texttt{mon-yyyy-string}] \texttt{"Mar-2020"}
\item[\texttt{dd-mon-yyyy-string}] \texttt{"15-Mar-2020"}
\item[\texttt{yyyy-mm-dd-string}] \texttt{"2020-3-15"}, also
  \texttt{iso-extended-date-string} and \texttt{iso-date-string}
\item[\texttt{yyyymmdd-string}] unpadded \texttt{YYYYMMDD}, also
  \texttt{iso-short-date-string}
\item[\texttt{date-time-string}] month, weekday (Monday~$=0$), year, and
  zero-padded hours, minutes, and seconds
\end{description}
